            <ArticleTitle>[Dysmenorrhea: patience, pills or hot-water bottle?].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2285-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Which treatments are used for dysmenorrhea and with what reported outcome? A questionnaire was sent to 2400 students and apprentices, following the &quot;retrospective treatment-outcome&quot; method. The response rate was 22%. Most frequent treatments used are ibuprofene (53%), paracetamol (51%), hormonal contraception (40%), hot-water bottle (or hot pad) (35%), food supplements or medicinal plants (23%). Physicians only discuss a tiny proportion of dysmenorrhea treatment in their consultation, because it is mostly a matter of self-treatment, with the family as the source of information in 80% of the cases. Rather surprising because not mentioned in most official guidelines, hot-water bottle (or hot pad) appears as the treatment followed by the best reported outcome (satisfactory in 92% of users).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
